    84	      "observation": "Here's the result of running `cat -n` on /workspace/paper.md:\n     1\t# **Bloat beneath Python\u2019s Scales:** **A Fine-Grained Inter-Project Dependency Analysis**\n     2\t\n     3\t[GEORGIOS-PETROS DROSOS,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0009-0007-2457-1421) Athens University of Economics and Business, Greece and ETH Zurich,\n     4\t\n     5\tSwitzerland\n     6\t\n     7\t[THODORIS SOTIROPOULOS,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0000-0002-9906-3073) ETH Zurich, Switzerland\n     8\t[DIOMIDIS SPINELLIS,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0000-0003-4231-1897) Athens University of Economics and Business, Greece and Delft University of\n     9\t\n    10\tTechnology, Netherlands\n    11\t[DIMITRIS MITROPOULOS,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0000-0002-5061-9018) University of Athens, Greece\n    12\t\n    13\t\n    14\tModern programming languages promote software reuse via package managers that facilitate the integration\n    15\tof inter-dependent software libraries. Software reuse comes with the challenge of dependency bloat, which\n    16\trefers to unneeded and excessive code incorporated into a project through reused libraries. Such bloat exhibits\n    17\tsecurity risks and maintenance costs, increases storage requirements, and slows down load times. In this work,\n    18\twe conduct a large-scale, fine-grained analysis to understand bloated dependency code in the PyPI ecosystem.\n    19\tOur analysis is the first to focus on different granularity levels, including bloated dependencies, bloated files,\n    20\tand bloated methods. This allows us to identify the specific parts of a library that contribute to the bloat. To do\n    21\tso, we analyze the source code of 1,302 popular Python projects and their 3,232 transitive dependencies. For\n    22\teach project, we employ a state-of-the-art static analyzer and incrementally construct the fine-grained project\n    23\tdependency graph (FPDG), a representation that captures all inter-project dependencies at method-level.\n    24\tOur reachability analysis on the FPDG enables the assessment of bloated dependency code in terms of several\n    25\taspects, including its prevalence in the PyPI ecosystem, its relation to software vulnerabilities, its root causes,\n    26\tand developer perception. Our key finding suggests that PyPI exhibits significant resource underutilization:\n    27\tmore than 50% of dependencies are bloated. This rate gets worse when considering bloated dependency code\n    28\tat a more subtle level, such as bloated files and bloated methods. Our fine-grained analysis also indicates\n    29\tthat there are numerous vulnerabilities that reside in bloated areas of utilized packages (15% of the defects\n    30\texisting in PyPI). Other major observations suggest that bloated code primarily stems from omissions during\n    31\tcode refactoring processes and that developers are willing to debloat their code: Out of the 36 submitted pull\n    32\trequests, developers accepted and merged 30, removing a total of 35 bloated dependencies. We believe that\n    33\tour findings can help researchers and practitioners come up with new debloating techniques and development\n    34\tpractices to detect and avoid bloated code, ensuring that dependency resources are utilized efficiently.\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\tCCS Concepts: \u2022 **Software and its engineering** \u2192 **Software libraries and repositories** .\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\tAdditional Key Words and Phrases: software bloat, dependencies, debloating, call graph, Python, PyPI\n    41\t\n    42\t\n    43\t**ACM Reference Format:**\n    44\t\n    45\tGeorgios-Petros Drosos, Thodoris Sotiropoulos, Diomidis Spinellis, and Dimitris Mitropoulos. 2024. Bloat\n    46\tbeneath Python\u2019s Scales: A Fine-Grained Inter-Project Dependency Analysis. Proc. ACM Softw. Eng. 1, FSE,\n    47\t[Article 114 (July 2024), 24 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3660821](https://doi.org/10.1145/3660821)\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\t[Authors\u2019 addresses: Georgios-Petros Drosos, Athens University of Economics and Business, Athens, Greece and ETH](https://orcid.org/0009-0007-2457-1421)\n    51\t[Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland, gdrosos@student.ethz.ch; Thodoris Sotiropoulos, ETH Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland, theodoros.](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9906-3073)\n    52\t[sotiropoulos@inf.ethz.ch; Diomidis Spinellis, Athens University of Economics and Business, Athens, Greece and Delft](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4231-1897)\n    53\t[University of Technology, Delft, Netherlands, dds@aueb.gr; Dimitris Mitropoulos, University of Athens, Athens, Greece,](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5061-9018)\n    54\tdimitro@ba.uoa.gr.\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\tThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t\u00a9 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\n    61\tACM 2994-970X/2024/7-ART114\n    62\t\n    63\t[https://doi.org/10.1145/3660821](https://doi.org/10.1145/3660821)\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\tProc. ACM Softw. Eng., Vol. 1, No. FSE, Article 114. Publication date: July 2024.\n    67\t\n    68\t\n    69\t114:2 Georgios-Petros Drosos, Thodoris Sotiropoulos, Diomidis Spinellis, and Dimitris Mitropoulos\n    70\t\n    71\t\n  <response clipped><NOTE>To save on context only part of this file has been shown to you. You should retry this tool after you have searched inside the file with `grep -n` in order to find the line numbers of what you are looking for.</NOTE>


